[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Conflict Resolution During Offline Reconnection in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building resilient web and mobile applications often requires robust offline support. Firebase Firestore provides out-of-the-box offline persistence, allowing users to read and write data locally when network connectivity is lost. However, when the client reconnects, conflicts can arise if the same document was modified both locally and on the server while offline. This tutorial explores strategies to manage and resolve these conflicts effectively."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Firestore Offline Persistence and Concurrency"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, Firestore uses a last-write-wins strategy based on server timestamps. When a client reconnects, any local mutations are replayed to the server. If another client updated the same document in the meantime, the last update applied to the server overwrites previous changes. While simple, this approach can lead to data loss in collaborative environments. To implement custom conflict resolution, you need to track metadata such as timestamps, user IDs, and version numbers."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Enabling Offline Persistence"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before handling conflicts, ensure that offline persistence is enabled in your Firestore configuration. For web applications using the modular SDK, use the initializeFirestore function with persistentLocalCache."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import { initializeFirestore, persistentLocalCache, persistentMultipleTabManager } from 'firebase/firestore';

const db = initializeFirestore(app, {
  localCache: persistentLocalCache({
    tabManager: persistentMultipleTabManager()
  })
});"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Strategies for Conflict Resolution"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Last-Write-Wins (Default): Suitable for non-critical data where the most recent operation is always correct."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Client-Side Merging: Comparing local changes with server state upon reconnection and merging arrays or fields programmatically."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Transaction-Based Resolution: Using Firestore transactions or Cloud Functions to evaluate business logic rules before committing updates."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Optimistic Concurrency Control"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent silent data overwriting, you can implement optimistic concurrency using a version field. Each update increments the version number. If the server version does not match the expected client version, a conflict is flagged."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"import { doc, runTransaction, serverTimestamp } from 'firebase/firestore';

async db => {
  const docRef = doc(db, 'documents', 'sampleId');

  try {
    await runTransaction(db, async (transaction) => {
      const sfDoc = await transaction.get(docRef);
      if (!sfDoc.exists()) {
        throw new Error('Document does not exist!');
      }

      const newPopulation = sfDoc.data().population + 1;
      transaction.update(docRef, { 
        population: newPopulation,
        lastUpdated: serverTimestamp()
      });
    });
    console.log('Transaction successfully committed!');
  } catch (e) {
    console.log('Transaction failed: ', e);
  }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Pending Writes in UI"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can detect whether data has been synced with the server by inspecting the metadata of a document snapshot. The hasPendingWrites property tells you if there are local modifications waiting to be sent to the backend."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import { doc, onSnapshot } from 'firebase/firestore';

onSnapshot(doc(db, 'cities', 'SF'), (doc) => {
  const source = doc.metadata.hasPendingWrites ? 'Local' : 'Server';
  console.log(Data came from ${source}, doc.data());
});"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Managing offline reconnection conflicts in Firebase Firestore requires a thoughtful balance between automatic persistence and explicit validation. By leveraging transactions, metadata tracking, and client-side state checks, you can ensure your applications maintain data integrity even in unstable network environments."}]
